    word_icons=['cloud', 'folder', 'gear', 'key', 'chart'],
    words_note='Five words for today, and each gets its own picture. A hosted model runs on the vendor’s computers, and you pay to use it. Open weights means the model itself can be downloaded and run where you choose, under a licence. Fine-tuning is further training on your own examples. Lock-in is being stuck with a supplier because leaving costs too much. A benchmark is a fixed public test. We start where Session 5 ended, with the chatbot itself.',
    cover_note='Welcome back. In Session 5 we opened up the chatbot: it writes one likely word at a time, and fluent is not the same as true. Today we step back from how it writes to who sells it to you. Every leader who buys AI meets the same three questions: which model and which supplier, where does our data go, and what will it cost to run? We start with a ruling in Kampala, map the makers, compare three routes, tour six industries, and watch two assistants answer the same customer complaint. Then you choose a route for each entry in your AI Opportunity Portfolio.',
    outcome_note='That question gives you three things to leave with. You will be able to say who makes the models you hear about, and tell a hosted model from an open-weights one in plain words. You will choose between renting, hosting and building on a partner by asking four questions: where the data goes and what the law says, the cost of each use, how hard it is to leave, and whether the supplier will last. And each portfolio entry will carry a route, a data note and an exit plan.',
    keep_note='Keep the take-away sheet. It has the three routes, the four questions, the vendor questions, and today’s recorded test: the hosted assistant passed 5 of 5 checks, the small open model 1 of 5 in each of three runs, on one task. Your portfolio now says, for each entry, which route, where the data goes and how you would leave. In Session 7 we take the tool you chose and learn to brief it well, because the same assistant gives very different work to a weak brief and a strong one.',
